  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will persist and increase this evening. A \nshortwave trough will lift northeast across the Great Basin and \ninto the Northern Rockies. At the base of the\u00a0trough a 90-110 knot\njet will prevail. Lift from the jet produced thunderstorms\u00a0this \nmorning over southern Utah. Expect lift from the right entrance \nregion to trigger scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \nlate this afternoon and more so this evening. Instability\u00a0will be \nlimited (less than 500 J/kg), as will moisture. Dew points will \nonly be in the 30s to lower 40s, but mid-level moisture will be \nbetter. A brief period of moderate rain is possible, but overall \nprecipitation is expected to be light.\u00a0\n\nFor Thursday through Sunday, there will be a longwave trough will\ncover the northwest part of the country and an upper-level high \nwill sit over the southeast. This will result in a southwest flow \naloft over Colorado. Temperatures will run above normal under this\npattern with highs in the upper 80s to mid 90s each day. Dry \nconditions are expected Thursday with precipitable water values \nslightly below normal. Moisture will slowly increase each day. For\nFriday isolated showers and thunderstorms will be possible over \nthe higher terrain. Saturday will see a slight chance for showers \nand storms spreading east onto the nearby plains. By Sunday, \nprecipitable water values reach 140-180 percent of normal. This \nincrease in moisture is expected to produce a round of scattered \nafternoon/evening thunderstorms.\n\nFor next week, the overall pattern over Colorado changes little \nas southwest flow aloft persists. The biggest question mark will \nbe how much moisture lifts northward into the region. Models \ngenerally place us on the edge of the better moisture, leading to \nan uncertain forecast. Scattered afternoon/evening showers and \nthunderstorms look possible each day at this time, but a slight \nshift in the pattern could bring much drier air into the region. \nTemperatures are expected to stay above normal with highs in the \nmid 80s to lower 90s.",
